+decision: redesign
+triggers: shared-cause, defect-type
+summary: Four r1 findings come from one decision. §Ring caps and drain policy uses the replay-ring caps as the flow-control mechanism, with acks sent on receipt and DATA unbounded. Replace it with one symmetric windowed flow-control rule. The rest stay local, swept as four rule-clusters plus four independent fixes.
+pattern: The flow-control decision ("ring full = backpressure", ACK on receipt, uncapped DATA) shows up four times. It surfaces as the keeper eagerly filling 16 MiB and losing coalescing ([Should-fix] Ring caps … "Leg up …"). It surfaces as an 8 MiB local overflow killing healthy sessions ([Should-fix] Ring caps … "Local upstream ring …"). It surfaces as uncapped receive-side buffering and a blocked bridge thread ([Should-fix] Session wire protocol Rule 3 …). It surfaces as heartbeats starved behind oversized DATA ([Nit] Session wire protocol Rule 6 …). Four other findings come from the keeper rendezvous/identity decision (shared temp_dir hashed name): the Blocking squat, the hash missing the server socket path, the build_id non sequitur and idle-exit ordering. Five come from leg-0 fallback and failure classification. Three come from the keeper's HELLO/session-state table, which has no resume marker and no server-connect-failure or parked-EOF case. These are clusters of one rule each and can be fixed locally within their sections. No recurrence: this is round 1.
