+changes: The flow-control decision in §Ring caps and drain policy, plus Session wire protocol Rule 3 (ACK = "received") and the DATA row (unbounded payload). Each replay ring's cap currently doubles as the only backpressure mechanism: asymmetric (keeper stops reading the server only when its 16 MiB ring is full; the local side has no leg-up rule and ends the session on 8 MiB overflow). Acks are sent on receipt, not delivery. The Keeper/ReplayRing/SessionEndpoint key-abstraction lines and the architecture diagram's keeper tuple restate it.
+to: A single symmetric flow-control rule in session.rs, applied identically by both ends. (1) DATA payload ≤ 64 KiB. Any inbound byte resets the 8 s silence timer, and HEARTBEAT/ACK are never queued behind more than one DATA chunk. (2) ACK means "delivered to the local consumer" (written to the client socket or the server socket), not merely received. Each end's receive hold-back is capped at one max herdr frame plus one DATA chunk. (3) While the leg is up, each sender keeps at most a fixed in-flight window (e.g. 256 KiB unacked) and stops reading its source (server socket at the keeper, client socket at the local bridge) when the window is full. That restores today's small-pipe backpressure and the server's render coalescing. The consumer write runs on its own thread so leg reads, heartbeats and should_stop polling never block. (4) While the leg is down, the sender keeps reading its source into the replay ring up to the ring cap (8 MiB local, 16 MiB keeper). Overflow ends the session. A single message larger than the ring during an outage is an explicit, documented session end. (5) Diagram and Key abstractions name exactly one outbound ring per end. This is a fixed window, not adaptive pacing.
