§Legs "One leg at a time. Back-off 1, 2, 4, 8, 8…"; Leg-0 failures, [Nit] §Discovery and fallback: "On leg 0, before any byte has reached the client, that connection is served by upstream's plain bridge_connection instead", [Nit] §Discovery and fallback (Unsupported detected by "unsupported remote client bridge option") vs "Fatal classification on leg 0 for --remote uses only reported_failure() and the exit status", [Nit] Keeper > Per HELLO: "A fresh token creates a session, connects to client_socket_path() and answers new."; WELCOME status enum, [Nit] Keeper 'Server EOF' and acceptance "A live handoff during a session delivers ServerShutdown to the client", [Nit] Out of scope: "Windows local clients may still resume against Unix remotes through crate::ipc" vs Rule 7 ("128 bits read from /dev/urandom"), Leg socketpair, acceptance "Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock are unchanged"
+scope: inside — it serves Main challenge 3 "Deliver exactly once in both directions … with bounded memory" and the Goal's "no lost or duplicated input". It does not touch the non-goal "QUIC, adaptive pacing and prediction (Phases 2–4)": the window is a fixed compile-time constant, not adaptive pacing, and the ~11-line upstream footprint is unchanged because everything lives in src/mosh/session.rs, ring.rs, local_bridge.rs and keeper.rs.
+recommendation: The four flow-control findings are one under-specified decision seen from four angles. Patching them one by one (keeper window here, local stop-reading there, ack-after-delivery elsewhere, DATA cap in a table row) risks an asymmetric, mutually inconsistent rule that resurfaces next round. Restating flow control once in session.rs costs about the same as the four patches and closes the whole class. Fix the other findings locally, swept by the clusters above so each rule's unflagged instances are fixed in the same pass.
